is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any packet loss between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and the partner host pc2 (200.1.1.2). Multiple targeted checks show the path is healthy end-to-end. ICMP from pc1 to pc2 returns 0% loss over repeated runs (including a 1472-byte DF test validating MTU), and router1-to-pc2 pings also show 0% loss. BGP between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is established and exchanging the LAN routes correctly; both routers’ RIBs/FIBs have the expected next-hops. Interface health is clean: no RX/TX errors or drops reported on any involved interfaces (router1/eth0, router1/eth1, router2/eth0, router2/eth1, and both hosts), and tc qdisc statistics show zero drops/ecn marks. No firewall rules are present on the routers (default ACCEPT), so ICMP isn’t being filtered in-transit. A TCP iperf from pc1 to pc2 achieved ~134–139 Mbps with only 9 retransmissions over 5 seconds, consistent with a clean link rather than the 30% loss described.

Given this evidence, the infrastructure is operating normally. The earlier “one-in-three” ping loss was likely a transient endpoint issue (CPU load, local firewall/ICMP rate-limiting, or test methodology) rather than a network fault. Corrective fix: none required on the network. Please re-test from pc1 using a sustained ping or iperf; if symptoms recur, collect an MTR/pcap from pc1 and verify endpoint CPU/firewall settings on both ends for transient ICMP/traffic throttling. 
=== END ===